{
  "concept": "A substance's phase is fixed entirely by where its (T, p) state point lies relative to the coexistence curves; the leftward-leaning melting line means pressure alone can melt ice, and which transitions a heating path crosses depends on the pressure at which it is run.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "title 'H2O' centered at top",
    "vertical axis 'Pressure, p (atm)' with nonlinear (log-like) ticks at 0.006, 1, 218",
    "horizontal axis 'Temperature, T (°C)' with nonlinear ticks at 0, 0.01, 100, 374",
    "red sublimation curve from lower-left up to the triple point",
    "red melting curve rising almost vertically from the triple point, leaning slightly LEFT (negative slope)",
    "red boiling (vaporization) curve sweeping up-right from the triple point to the critical point",
    "filled dot 'Triple point' at (0.01 °C, 0.006 atm) with dashed guide lines to both axes",
    "filled dot 'Critical point' at (374 °C, 218 atm) with dashed guide lines to both axes",
    "dashed guide pair marking the (100 °C, 1 atm) crossing on the boiling curve",
    "italic region label 's' (solid) left of the melting curve",
    "italic region label 'l' (liquid) in the wedge between melting and boiling curves",
    "italic region label 'v' (vapor) right of / below the boiling and sublimation curves",
    "curved-arrow tag 'Melting' pointing at the melting curve",
    "curved-arrow tag 'Boiling' pointing at the boiling curve",
    "curved-arrow tag 'Sublimation' pointing at the sublimation curve",
    "movable state-point marker (dot) positioned by the T and p sliders",
    "translucent highlight tint over whichever region (s, l, or v) contains the state point",
    "horizontal heating-path line drawn at the pressure selected by the path toggle, with transition markers where it crosses coexistence curves"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "temperature",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Temperature T (°C)",
      "range": [-10, 400, 1],
      "default": 25,
      "teaches": "Moving the state point horizontally changes phase only when it crosses a coexistence curve — phase is read off location, not off temperature alone"
    },
    {
      "id": "pressure",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pressure p (atm)",
      "range": [0, 250, 0.5],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "Raising pressure at a fixed sub-zero temperature pushes the state point across the left-leaning melting line, flipping the phase from solid to liquid — added pressure melts ice"
    },
    {
      "id": "heatingPath",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Heating path (constant p)",
      "options": ["1 atm (normal)", "Low pressure (sublimation)", "Supercritical (>218 atm)"],
      "default": "1 atm (normal)",
      "teaches": "The same heating (rising T) produces melting then boiling at 1 atm, sublimation at low pressure, and no sharp transition above the critical pressure — the path's pressure decides which boundaries are crossed"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Phase = location",
      "narration": "Drag the temperature and pressure sliders: the state point moves and the solid, liquid, or vapor region tints beneath it. Wherever the point sits relative to the three red curves determines the phase.",
      "state": { "temperature": 25, "pressure": 1, "heatingPath": "1 atm (normal)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pressure melts ice",
      "narration": "Hold temperature just below 0 °C and raise the pressure slider. The point crosses the leftward-leaning melting curve and the highlighted region flips from solid to liquid — because that boundary has a negative slope, squeezing ice turns it to water.",
      "state": { "temperature": -1, "pressure": 1, "heatingPath": "1 atm (normal)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Normal heating at 1 atm",
      "narration": "At 1 atm the horizontal path crosses the melting line near 0 °C (solid to liquid) and the boiling line at 100 °C (liquid to vapor) — the everyday sequence of melting then boiling.",
      "state": { "temperature": 25, "pressure": 1, "heatingPath": "1 atm (normal)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Low-pressure heating: sublimation",
      "narration": "Drop the path below the triple-point pressure (0.006 atm). The horizontal line now crosses only the sublimation curve, so solid turns directly to vapor with no liquid stage.",
      "state": { "temperature": 25, "pressure": 0.006, "heatingPath": "Low pressure (sublimation)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Supercritical heating",
      "narration": "Raise the path above 218 atm, past the critical point. The line crosses no coexistence curve at all: liquid and vapor merge into one supercritical fluid, so there is no sharp boiling transition.",
      "state": { "temperature": 380, "pressure": 230, "heatingPath": "Supercritical (>218 atm)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Phase is not a property of temperature or pressure alone but of the (T, p) point's position relative to the coexistence curves — which is why a negatively sloped melting line lets pressure melt ice, and why the same heating produces boiling, sublimation, or nothing depending on the pressure of the path.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Render as an SVG diagram with nonlinear (visually log-like) axes: y ticks 0.006, 1, 218 spaced so 1 sits low and 218 near the top; x ticks 0, 0.01, 100, 374 with 0.01 hugging 0 and 100/374 spread right. Draw the three coexistence curves in a single red stroke meeting at a black filled 'Triple point' dot (0.01 °C, 0.006 atm) and the boiling branch terminating at a black filled 'Critical point' dot (374 °C, 218 atm); the melting branch must visibly lean LEFT of vertical. Add gray dashed guide lines from both dots to the axes and a dashed pair at (100 °C, 1 atm). Place italic serif 's', 'l', 'v' in the left, central-wedge, and right regions. Tag the curves with small curved arrows labeled Melting, Boiling, Sublimation. Overlay a draggable-looking state dot driven by the two sliders, tint the containing region with a soft translucent fill (e.g. blue-ish for l, gray for s, orange for v), and draw the constant-pressure heating path as a horizontal line with small markers + labels at each curve crossing. Keep axis labels and the H2O title in the original bold styling."
}

